Last edit: \f1 05\f0 -\f1 Aug\f0 -2015 cjl\par
\par
How to add a new program source file \f1 for\f0  develop\f1 ment\f0  with the P?S Advanced SIMH package.\par
\par
Additional topics include related issues with regard to OS/8 and P?S/8 as used with\f1  \f0 the SIMH PDP-8 simulator and resolution of problems whe\f1 re\f0  feasible.\par
\par
Newer versions of this document may add additional topics as required.  Feedback is\f1  \f0 welcome on any related topic.\par
Contact Charles Lasner CLASystems@gmail.com for any\f1  \f0 questions.\par
\par
Technical level:\f1\tab\f0 Moderate to high with respect to Windows/MS-DOS as \f1\tab\tab\tab\tab\f0 well as PDP-8\f1  \f0 operating environments such as OS/8 \f1\tab\tab\tab\tab\f0 and P?S/8.\par
\par
The main purpose of this document is to explain how to organize the components\f1  \f0 needed to support easy access to the rest of this package\f1  for the purposes of\f0  develop\f1 ing\f0  a new source\f1  \f0 file\f1  project\f0 .\f1  \f0 Consequential issues that crop up during development will also be discussed.\par
\par
Note: In very few instances, extremely long complete pathnames are indicated to be\f1  \f0 explicitly used as part of a command file or\f1  \f0 parameter.  For readability purposes\f1  \f0 only, these are shown on multiple lines of this document with a \cf1\highlight2\f1\'a7\cf0\highlight0\f0  symbol\f1 .  T\f0 he actual\f1  \f0 usage must be one long line; a single \highlight3\f1 <SP>\highlight0\f0  character should be used where the \cf1\highlight2\f1\'a7\cf0\highlight0  \f0 is show\f1 n\f0 .\par
\par
Advanced SIMH Package Drive designation.\par
\par
A valid volume and drive letter must be chosen to operate the \f1 P?S \f0 Advanced SIMH package.\f1   \f0 Depending on system complexity, this will be C: or any valid drive through Z:  The\f1  \f0 choice will be set either manually within Windows' environment variables section or\f1  \f0 using available batch command files within the overall \f1 P?S \f0 Advanced SIMH package.  Due\f1  \f0 to the way various command lines function within MS-DOS and Windows, the various\f1  \f0 shortcuts and batch files depend on a master environment variable for proper\f1  \f0 functionality.\par
\par
Depending on the context, the string\par
\par
\i %PDP-8_DRIVE%\i0\par
\par
is to be typed literally as stated.  This applies to all command lines and input to\f1  \f0 shortcut fields, etc.\page Advanced SIMH Drive textual reference.\par
\par
When the reference to the drive is made as part of ordinary text within this\f1  \f0 document, it refers to the drive letter [and a ":" character] chosen for the actual\f1  \f0 drive used to install the \f1 P?S \f0 Advanced SIMH package such as D:.  For example, the\f1  \f0 COROUTINE.PAL file described in the next section will be located on a volume on the\par
target system such as drive D:.  However, the path to all such files is described\f1  \f0 throughout this document starting with %PDP-8_DRIVE%.  Since this is merely\f1  \f0 documentation, not the contents of a command file or parameter specification,\f1  \f0 mentally substitute the drive letter [and ":"] chosen for installation of the\f1  \f0 Advanced SIMH package.\par
\par
Project files within the \f1 P?S \f0 Advanced SIMH environment.\par
\par
Various source files can be developed within the \f1 P?S \f0 Advanced SIMH environment.  To make\f1  \f0 this process significantly easier, various shortcuts and batch files can be created\f1  \f0 to avoid needless tedium.\par
\par
As an example, we will use the existing file COROUTINE.PAL. [Note: COROUTINE.PAL is\f1  \f0 actually an excerpt from the source file for Kermit-12, a Kermit implementation for\f1  \f0 OS/8 written by Charles Lasner [with some help from others].  It was extracted as an\f1  \f0 example of a well-constructed practical coroutine for illustrative use in an\f1  \f0 academic setting to explain PDP-8 coroutines used in non-real-time programs.\par
\par
The\f1  \f0 program segment is meant to be used to obtain unpacked bytes from an OS/8 input file\f1  \f0 called as a conventional subroutine from a main program; the coroutine aspect\f1  of things\f0  is\f1  \f0 totally contained within the called code including certain mode settings that could\f1  \f0 be set externally such as seven bit ASCII text or eight bit binary mode.\par
\par
Non-real-time coroutines were chosen to introduce the general concept of coroutines\f1  \f0 to students who had\f1  no prior experience with this data structure\f0 ; real-time usage of coroutines is\f1  a\f0  no\f1 n-\f0 trivial matter.  It is \f1 far\f0  more difficult to illustrate how coroutines facilitate\f1  \f0 foreground processing or inter-task communications without presenting a\f1  \f0 completely\f1  \f0 detailed entire program.  The academic setting was meant to introduce students to\f1  \f0 coroutines, not cause them to run for the nearest exit.\par
\par
The COROUTINE.PAL file is located within one of the recommended source file\f1  \f0 directories, in this case\f1 :\par
\par
\f0 %PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\Source Files\\Generic Source Files\par
\par
\f1 which is used for source file projects not particularly associated with any particular operating system [such as OS/8 or P?S/8].\f0\par
\par
[Note: The\f1  \f0 environment variable PDP-8_DRIVE should already be setup by the package setup\f1  \f0 program as documented elsewhere.  It should be set to D: or some similar drive\f1  \f0 letter\f1  other than C: if at all possible\f0 .]\page Important: After\f1  first setting up the PDP-8_DRIVE environment variable\f0 , the Windows system should be logged off or rebooted\f1  \f0 at least once before performing any of the operations described below to allow the\f1  \f0 environment variable change to take effect.\par
\par
The objective is to create two batch files to support the source file, each with a\f1  \f0 shortcut to their respective batch file, such as:\par
\par
1)\f1\tab\f0 Edit COROUTINE.PAL - This is a batch file that runs a\f1  designated \tab editing program to modify\f0  the \f1 source \f0 file\f1  [which in\f0  this example\f1  \tab is the Windows standard\f0  Notepad.exe]\f1 ,\f0  and then pass the\f1  \f0 file to \f1\tab\f0 the SIMH PDP-8 simulator by creating a copy as the paper-tape \f1\tab\f0 input\f1  \f0 stream file called PTRFILE.TXT.\f1   \f0 [Note: \f1 Nearly a\f0 ll \f1\tab\f0 files \f1\tab\f0 used in the SIMH PDP-8\f1  \f0 simulator use arbitrary names.\f1   \f0 By \f1\tab\f0 changing the\f1  content of the\f0  config.ini \f1\tab\f0 file\f1 [s]\f0 , alternate file\f1  \tab names \f0 can be defined to \f1\tab\f0 suit personal preference.]\par
\par
\f1\tab If the source file editor is closed [which closes the batch \tab file], the SIMH PDP-8 simulator can be run separately if \tab desired; however, as long as the source file is updated, it is \tab not necessary to end the editing session.  [see 2) below.]\f0\par
\par
2)\f1\tab\f0 Copy and Go for COROUTINE.PAL - This is a batch file that also \f1\tab\f0 copies the file\f1  \f0 to PTRFILE.TXT as in 1) above, then runs the SIMH \f1\tab\f0 PDP-8 simulator to allow the\f1  \f0 high-speed reader handler of OS/8 to \f1\tab\f0 create the file within the OS/8 environment.\f1   As source file \tab edits are made, this batch file can be used as need be.\par
\par
\tab The PDP-8 operating system of choice [OS/8 or P?S/8] is set \tab within the batch file by setting the GOCONFIG environment \tab variable as shown in the example below.\f0\par
\par
\f1\tab [Note: Future releases of P?S/8 will include the PTUTIL utility. \tab This will allow P?S/8 to process passed input files directly.  \tab The present method is to first use OS/8 configured with paper-\tab tape reader support; after the file is in the OS/8 environment, \tab the P?S/8 OS8CON program can convert the OS/8 data into P?S/8 \tab files.]\f0\par
\par
Example batch files.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Edit and Copy the COROUTINE.PAL file.  The COROUTINE.PAL file is \f1\tab\f0 open in Notepad and\f1  \f0 then copied into the paper-tape input stream \f1\tab\f0 file PTRFILE.TXT\f1  afterwards\f0 .  When the editing\f1  \f0 session completes, \f1\tab\f0 it would \f1\tab\f0 be appropriate to run the SIMH PDP-8 simulator\f1  booting \tab the operating system of choice [OS/8 or P?S/8]\f0 .\page File Name: Edit and Copy COROUTINE.PAL.BA\f1 T\par
\par
\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\par
REM Edit a specific file then copy it into the PTR: file in\par
\f1 REM \f0 preparation for\f1  \f0 assembly in SIMH.\par
\par
REM Last edit: \f1 05\f0 -\f1 Aug\f0 -2015 cjl\par
\par
REM Set the file name.\par
\par
SET FILENAME=COROUTINE.PAL\par
\par
REM Set the path for source files.\par
\par
SET SRCPATH=%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\Source Files\\Generic Source Files\par
\par
REM Set the path for the working files.\par
\par
SET WORKPATH=%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH_Working_Files\par
\par
START /WAIT NOTEPAD.EXE "%SRCPATH%\\%FILENAME%"\par
\par
REM Copy the source file to the SIMH PTR: file.\par
\par
COPY /Y "%SRCPATH%\\%FILENAME%" "%WORKPATH%\\PTRFILE.TXT"\fs20\par
\par
\f1\fs24\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\fs17\par
\f0\fs24 For a new source file, the FILENAME and SRCPATH lines must be changed.  Alternatives\f1  \f0 to Notepad.exe can be used [\f1 specifying \f0 an explicit path as required].\par
\par
The next batch file will copy the COROUTINE.PAL file to the paper-tape input stream\f1  \f0 file PTRFILE.TXT.  It is presumed a\f1  host\f0  editing session \f1 might be\f0  in progress which need not\f1  \f0 be terminated.  The SIMH PDP-8 simulator will be run booting to\f1  either\f0  OS/8\f1  or P?S/8 as determined by the setting of the GOCONFIG environment variable within the batch file.\f0\par
\par
Eventually, after looking at results obtained from the SIMH PDP-8 simulator session\f1  \f0 [such as PTPFILE.TXT or LPTOUT.TXT after appropriate filtering and stripping has\f1  \f0 modified the file to be acceptable to the host environment], further editing \f1 can be \f0 performed without necessarily terminating the editing session.\par
\par
Note: OS/8 users have a choice of which output stream to pass the results back\f1  \f0 through.  P?S/8 users should use LPTOUT.TXT due to limited support of the high-speed\f1  \f0 punch within the present release of P?S/8 associated with the Advanced SIMH PDP-8\f1  \f0 simulator package at the time of this writing.\page\f1 The developers of \f0 P?S/8\f1  have\f0  always avoided\f1  \f0 paper-tape hardware\f1  support\f0  for a variety of reasons:\par
\par
\f1\tab a)\tab Using paper-tape is i\f0 ncredibly inefficient and \f1 was \f0 wasteful\f1  \tab\tab\f0 on the actual \f1 computer \f0 hardware\f1  used back\f0  in the day,\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 b)\f1\tab N\f0 o high-speed\f1  paper-tape\f0  hardware was available to\f1  the\f0  P?S/8\f1\tab\tab\f0 developers; only the slow and quite unreliable\f1  Teletype \tab\tab\tab Model 33 \tab ASR \f0 was available on\f1  the specific\f0  PDP-8 systems\f1  \tab\tab\tab used to test and develop P?S/8.\f0\par
\par
\f1\tab\tab\f0 In fact,\f1  the original system used for testing and \tab\tab\tab\tab development had a Teletype Model 35 KSR\f0  as the system \f1\tab\tab\tab\tab\f0 console; \f1 as such, no paper-tape capability existed on that \tab\tab\tab system.\par
\f0\par
Future releases of P?S/8 \f1 will\f0  include\f1  the PTUTUL\f0  \f1 program\f0  \f1\tab\f0 supporting\f1  ASCII text \f0 paper-tape devices\f1  including the device 01 high-speed reader and device 02 high-speed punch, and the Teletype Model 33 ASR and Model 35 ASR console terminals which can read and punch paper-tape at low speed only.\par
\par
The paper-tape support is being added \f0 primarily as a concession to the SIMH PDP-8 simulator\f1  environment\f0 ; the\f1  \f0 high-speed paper-tape reader and \f1 high-speed paper-tape \f0 punch is the primary device used to bring files in\f1  \f0 and out of the simulated environment to allow \f1\tab\f0 host utilities to operate on the files\f1  \f0 as well as using PDP-8 \f1\tab\f0 facilities.\f1   PTUTIL will allow the reading and creation of P?S/8 TFS and extended-length files when completed.  [Note: PTUTIL for P?S/8 Version 8P may not support extended-length files due to the primitive suport inherent in this early release.]\f0\page File Name: Copy and Go COROUTINE.PAL.BAT\par
\par
\f1\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\par
\f1 @ECHO OFF\f0\par
\par
REM Copy the source file to the SIMH PTR: file and then run SIMH.\par
\par
REM Last edit: \f1 05\f0 -\f1 Aug\f0 -2015 cjl\par
\par
\f1 SETLOCAL\par
\par
REM Set desired PDP-8 bootup file below; REM the other one.\par
\par
SET GOCONFIG=os8config.ini\par
REM SET GOCONFIG=pqsconfig.ini\f0\par
\par
REM Set the file name.\par
\par
SET FILENAME=COROUTINE.PAL\par
\par
REM Set the path for source files.\par
\par
SET SRCPATH=%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\Source Files\\Generic Source Files\par
\par
REM Set the path for the working files.\par
\par
SET WORKPATH=%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH_Working_Files\par
\par
REM Copy the source file to the SIMH PTR: file.\par
\par
COPY /Y "%SRCPATH%\\%FILENAME%" "%WORKPATH%\\PTRFILE.TXT"\par
\par
REM Now run the SIMH PDP-8 Simulator.  If running OS/8, read\f1  the PTR:\f0\par
REM into a .PA or other text file using OS/8 PIP.\f1   If running P?S/8,\f0\par
REM use PTUTIL to read the PTR: input and\f1  \f0 create P?S/8 output files.\par
\par
CD "%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH_Working_Files"\par
\par
"%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH Simulator Releases\\PDP8.exe"\par
\cf1\highlight2\f1\'a7\cf0\highlight0\f0  "%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH Configuration Files\\%GOCONFIG%"\par
\par
\f1\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\par
Note: The\f1  \f0 last\f1  file\f0  line\f1  is\f0  shown in two parts\f1  separated by \cf1\highlight2\'a7\cf0\highlight0\f0  for\f1  \f0 readability purposes only\f1 ;  it should be one continuous line with a single \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  character between the two sections.\f0\par
\par
For a new source file, the FILENAME and SRCPATH lines must be changed.\par
\par
Both batch files should be stored in the\par
\par
%PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH Batch Files\par
\par
directory to support the new source file.\page Creating proper shortcuts for the new source file.\par
\par
