\par
Passing files from the host environment to the SIMH PDP-8 simulator [and back].\par
\par
Assuming the file is setup by a Copy and Go routine, the following should be\f1  \f0 executed within the SIMH PDP-8 simulator OS/8 environment [the COROUTINE.PAL file is\f1  \f0 the subject of this example]:\par
\par
.R PIP\par
*COROUT.PA<PTR:\par
^\cf3 [Press any key to "start" the "reader".]\cf0\f1 *\page\f0 Note: OS/8 only allows \f1 six\f0  character names with two character extensions in\f1  \f0 upper-case.  When the P?S/8 SHELL is completed and a similar utility is available,\f1  \f0 files will be allowed with up to 12 character names with up to four charact\f1 e\f0 r\f1  \f0 extensions.  The file name case will be retained for all cosmetic purposes but will\f1  \f0 be otherwise treated as caseless.  Thus, in the P?S/8 SHELL environment, the entire\par
file name could generally be used without truncation.]\par
\par
The file can then be used in the OS/8 environment and edited with TECO.SV if\f1  \f0 desired.  PAL8 can be used to assemble the file and create a listing output file.\f1  \f0 A typical usage might create COROUT.BN as the binary file and COROU\f1 T\f0 .LS as the\f1  \f0 listing file intended for printing or other purpose.\par
\par
Any file created within the OS/8 environment can be brought into the host\f1  \f0 environment as a file:\par
\par
.R PIP\par
*PTP:<COROUT.LS\par
\par
When the SIMH PDP-8 simulator is closed, utilities can remove\f1  \f0 artifacts making the\f1  \f0 result\f1 ing\f0  file acceptable to the host\par
Windows/MS-DOS standards.\par
\par
Only one file at a time can be returned to the host environment.  As such, it may be\f1  \f0 desirable to only edit in the host environment.  TECOC for Windows is available\f1  \f0 within the SIMH PDP-8 simulator Utilities directory; it is used as part of the\f1  \f0 filtering utilities needed to remove artifacts.\par
\par
The editing method used in this example involves the use of the\f1  \f0 standard Notepad.exe\f1  \f0 program within Windows.  All non-printing\f1  \f0 characters other than the exact sequence\f1  \highlight2 <CR>\highlight0\f0  immediately follows by\f1  \highlight2 <LF>\highlight0\f0  show as a thin rectangle with no way to distinguish\f1  \f0 the exact\f1  \f0 character.  Listing files will show pages separated by the \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0\f0  character\f1  \f0 in this manner.  The character can be copied and pasted within Notepad to insert\f1  \f0 characters where desired; however, it is the user's responsibility to ensure that\f1  \f0 no other non-printing characters are present.  Editors such as SEDT or Notepad++\f1  \f0 may be used if desired to reveal the codes of characters accidentally placed in the\f1  \f0 file.  Appropriate shortcuts should be created in lieu of those described above if\f1  \f0 desired.\page Optional P?S/8 development.\par
\par
Also provided in this package\f1  are\f0  release\f1 s\f0  of P?S/8 circa 197\f1 6 [Version 8P] and 1989 [Version 8Z] with minor binary patches applied in 2015 to fix longstanding bugs until a new source code-based release is available tentatively to be known as Version 9A.  In certain ways, P?S/8 is superior to OS/8 [and admittedly there are tradeoffs between the systems presently].  For more information about issues related to OS/8 and P?S/8 [including historical information about early development of both including the R-L Monitor] consult Appendix A of this document.\par
\par
\f0 Note: P?S/8 is currently being developed using the SIMH PDP-8 simulator package as\f1  \f0 described in this document.  Updated files will be made generally available as the\f1  \f0 author sees fit; this will include interim updates as well as complete releases [or\f1  \f0 patched versions] as they occur.\par
\par
\f1 P?S/8 source files are maintained in the host environment and  presently assembled in OS/8.  Once a rudimentary implementation of  the P?S/8 SHELL is completed, further development will be performed entirely in the SHELL environment [without OS/8].\f0\par
\par
Source files may be freely converted from the OS/8 environment to/from P?S/8 using\f1  \f0 prevailing conversion programs in P?S/8.  More\f1  \f0 documentation is available elsewhere\f1  \f0 regarding file conversion both in the present system and in future releases of both\f1  \f0 the basic P?S/8 system and the optional SHELL environment.\par
\par
Note: The conversion\f1  \f0 programs depend on specific configurations of the PDP-8 as carried out within the\f1  \f0 SIMH PDP-8 simulator configuration as used in this package.  Where possible, the\f1  \f0 normal handlers associated with P?S/8 will be compatible with OS/8 a\f1 t\f0  the physical\f1  \f0 block level.\par
\par
In certain cases, it will be necessary for the P?S/8 SHELL to use\par
special handlers geared towards OS/8 access to certain devices where P?S/8 uses a\f1  \f0 different method of access [generally to provide more storage on the same hardware;\f1  \f0 many PDP-8 peripherals can be used in a manner that wastes 1/3 of the available\f1  \f0 space.  OS/8 generally uses the inefficient operational modes on these devices due\f1  \f0 to lack of proper code space within the meager OS/8 handlers.  P?S/8 handlers may be\f1  \f0 provided for either mode depending on hardware and configuration specifics.  Only\f1  \f0 certain specific inter-system configurations will be acceptable to the conversion\f1  \f0 programs.  when the SHELL conversion program is written [tentatively to be known as\f1  \f0 PIPOS8] certain configuration rules can be somewhat relaxed.]\page\par
File Conversion quirks and remedies.\par
\par
P?S/8 ASCII text paper-tape issues.\par
\par
P?S/8 was written in an environment devoid of paper-tape, universally despised by\f1  \f0 all serious PDP-8 programmers.  Yet, in the SIMH PDP-8 simulator environment, the\f1  \f0 only way to readily communicate files between the SIMH simulated PDP-8 and the host\f1  \f0 environment is the use of the high-speed reader and punch handlers simulated as the\f1  \f0 contents of two files in the host environment.\par
\par
\f1 Until a P?S/8-based ASCII paper-tape utility is written [tenatively to be named PTUTIL], it will be necessary to use OS/8's high-speed reader [and perhaps punch] device handlers to bring source files into and out of the SIMH environment for further editing and other work in the host environment [Windows].\par
\par
Each available implementation has a unique utility for conversion between OS/8 and P?S/8.  The companion P?S/8 command guide document details the method available to both systems.\par
\par
Using the 1976 release of P?S/8 [Version 8P], the HR\{eader\} command can be used to create P?S/8 TFS text files directly; unfortunately, the lengths of the files must be carefully controlled to avoid loss.\par
\par
Note: The HR\{eader\} command was dropped in later implementations of\par
P?S/8.\par
\par
When the P?S/8 SHELL is implemented, utilities similar to PTUTIL will be available for a more direct conversion process.\f0\par
\par
P?S/8 binary paper-tape issues.\par
\par
P?S/8 supports binary paper-tape data using built-in command-line options of the BIN\f1  \f0 utility.  [The exact functionality is determined by proper selection of command-line\f1  \f0 option switches; BIN program documentation is available \f1 in other documents\f0 .]  This includes\par
the following capabilities:\par
\par
1)\f1\tab\f0 Loading existing binary TFS files such as are created by \f1\tab\f0 assembling source\f1  \f0 programs with P?S/8 PAL; various loading and \f1\tab\f0 startup options are set by\f1  \f0 command-line options.\par
\par
2)\f1\tab\f0 Launching a [slightly modified] BIN [and RIM] loader with built-\f1\tab\f0 in system reboot\f1  \f0 by restarting at 07600.  Both the low-speed and \f1\tab\f0 high-speed reader are supported.\page\f1\tab\f0 Note: The modifications to BIN are consistent with the usual \f1\tab\f0 implementation of\f1  \f0 the RIM loader; the command-line options \f1\tab\f0 determine which reader device is used in\f1  \f0 both loaders; no purpose \f1\tab\f0 is served using the front panel switch register toselect the \f1\tab\f0 input device since the BIN and RIM loaders are being launched \f1\tab\f0 from a\f1  storage-based \f0 operating system.  This is especially \f1\tab\f0 helpful in the SIMH PDP-8 simulator\f1  \f0 environment since the switch \f1\tab\f0 register must be set by unwieldy keyboard commands\f1  \f0 that suspend \f1\tab\f0 the SIMH simulator.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Unlike the DEC version of the BIN loader, P?S/8 can be restarted \f1\tab\f0 at 07600 while\f1  \f0 the BIN and RIM loaders are in memory [assuming \f1\tab\f0 the bootstrap code is not\f1  \f0 destroyed by the program loaded by BIN \f1\tab\f0 or RIM].\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Note: Restarting P?S/8 replaces\f1  \f0 the contents of 07600-07777 with \f1\tab\f0 the P?S/8 system kernel which, in turn, loads in\f1  \f0 the P?S/8 \f1\tab\f0 keyboard monitor and integrated editor.  The BIN and RIM loaders \f1\tab\f0 are\f1  \f0 provided merely as an accommodation to stand-alone paper-tape \f1\tab\f0 loading when\f1  \f0 [infrequently] needed; this is necessary to load \f1\tab\f0 "rogue" programs known to\f1  \f0 violate PDP-8 operating system \f1\tab\f0 conventions such as several DEC PDP-8/E diagnostic\f1  \f0 programs.  For \f1\tab\f0 al\f1 l\f0  conforming programs, loading should be accomplished within\par
\f1  \tab\f0 P?S/8 by loading binary files on the storage device.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 As with all other versions of the BIN loader, the standard \f1\tab\f0 restart address of\f1  \f0 07777 applies; all known versions of the RIM \f1\tab\f0 loader must be restarted at 07756;\f1  \f0 the specified loader [RIM or \f1\tab\f0 BIN] is automatically started by executing the\f1  \f0 command-line with \f1\tab\f0 appropriate loading options.  The BIN utility supports several\par
\f1\tab\f0 user memory pre-loading options using command-line options that \f1\tab\f0 can be performed\f1  \f0 before starting the selected loader.\par
\par
3)\f1\tab\f0 Reading binary paper tapes and converting to multiple binary TFS \f1\tab\f0 files including\f1  \f0 support for multiple sections within a single \f1\tab\f0 tape.  Both the low-speed and\f1  \f0 high-speed readers are supported in \f1\tab\f0 a choice of BIN or RIM format.\par
\par
4)\f1\tab\f0 Reading existent binary TFS files on the storage device and \f1\tab\f0 punching the contents\f1  \f0 to the low-speed or high-speed \f1 punch\f0  in \f1\tab\f0 either BIN or RIM format.\par
\par
Note: Thi\f1 s \f0 implementation of RIM format includes a checksum allowing the tapes to be read using either BIN or RIM loaders.\par
\par
OS/8 paper-tape and line-printer handler issues.\par
\par
The OS/8 paper-tape output handler exhibits bizarre quirks creating unexpected\f1  \f0 conversion difficulties.\page Note: documentation of part of this problem is so complex\f1  \f0 there is under development a separate "white paper" document on this problem.\par
Th\f1 e \f0 problems exist by design despite wide-spread ignorance of the problem.  [The\f1  \f0 problems are quite apparent in the host environment when outputting ASCII text files\f1  \f0 through the PTP: handler.\f1 ]\f0\par
\par
Certain of these problems are shared by the OS/8 device 66 standard LPT: handler;\f1  \f0 some of the problems actually originate in the system programs used; the LPT:\f1  \f0 handler can magnify the severity of the problem.\par
\par
P?S/8 line-printer issues.\par
\par
P?S/8 does not output to the paper-tape device [other than binary files].  The\f1  \f0 standard device 66 line-printer is used, which also creates several quirks.\par
\par
Note:\f1  \f0 Some of these problems are considered bugs associated with the 197\f1 6\f0  release of P?S/8\f1  \f0 fixed in later releases.\par
\par
Fixing problems using file filtering.\par
\par
All of the problems stated above [and more] have been overcome through the use of a\f1  \f0 filtering utility based on TECOC [TECO for Windows and MS-DOS] and TECO macros\f1  \f0 written by Charles Lasner.  Shortcuts to the utility exist in the Command directory\f1  \f0 of the \f1 P?S \f0 Advanced SIMH PDP-8 simulator package; both paper-tape and device 66 line\f1  \f0 printer output filtering are supported.\par
\par
Filtering details and issues.\par
\par
The basic purpose of filtering output files is to strip the high-order bit of all\f1  \f0 ASCII text characters since the host environment does not tolerate mark parity set;\f1  \f0 the host environment reserves this bit for output characters beyond the scope of\f1  \f0 both P?S/8 and OS/8.\par
\par
However, there are a variety of additional problems beyond this [some of which \f1 were \f0 totally unexpected].  Some logistic details of certain discovered problems follows:\par
\par
1)\f1\tab\f0 A widespread problem of many programs in both operating system \f1\tab\f0 stems from the\f1  \f0 inconsistent use of \highlight2\f1 <CR>\highlight0\f0  not followed by \highlight2\f1 <LF>\highlight0\f0  or \f1\tab\highlight2 <LF>\highlight0  \f0 not preceded by \highlight2\f1 <CR>\highlight0\f0 .\f1   \f0 Additionally, other context-dependent \f1\tab\f0 problems exist that must be handled in the\f1  \f0 proper order of \f1\tab\f0 discovery after these problems are noticed.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Note: Certain\f1  \f0 fixes are extremely pragmatic.\par
\par
2)\f1\tab\f0 Several "varieties" of unexpected artifacts are present in the \f1\tab\f0 output of PAL8\f1  \f0 [and to a far lesser extent the output of P?S/8 \f1\tab\f0 PAL also exhibits certain\f1  \f0 additional quirks].  Pragmatic code in \f1\tab\f0 the filtering utilities handle all known\f1  \f0 quirks in the output \f1\tab\f0 associated with both operating systems.\page 3)\f1\tab\f0 Once all detected artifacts are removed from a PAL8 output file, \f1\tab any l\f0 eading \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0  \f0 character[s] will be removed.  Modern printing \f1\tab\f0 techniques performed in the host\f1  \f0 environment include various \f1\tab\f0 forms of post-processing; initial blank pages will be\f1  \f0 eliminated.  \par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Note: \f1 In past years\f0  having an initial \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0\f0  made sense\f1 : a) \f0 it is \f1\tab\f0 not\f1  \f0 possible to determine the prior state of the li\f1 n\f0 e\f1  \f0 printer\f1 , b) \tab\f0 the previous page may\f1  \f0 not be set to top-of-form\f1  and c) \f0 there \f1\tab\f0 could be a\f1  \f0 partially printed page that must be\f1  \f0 ejected before \f1\tab\f0 starting \f1 the current \f0 print job.  P\f1 rint\f0  management has been \f1\tab\f0 transferred to\f1  \f0 the host \f1\tab\f0 operating system to handle all such \f1\tab\f0 printing issues during\f1  \f0 post-processing operations beyond the SIMH \f1\tab\f0 PDP-8 simulator environment.]\par
\par
4)\f1\tab\f0 The opening content of the 197\f1 6\f0  P?S/8 PAL output files is a bit \f1\tab\f0 more complicated\f1  \f0 than merely a single \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0\f0  character [after \f1\tab\f0 removal of all quirky output in the\f1  \f0 files created by both \f1\tab\f0 operating systems].  As of this writing, the latest\f1  \f0 filtering \f1\tab\f0 utilities remove the specific sequence; thus, there is basic \f1\tab\f0 parity in\f1  \f0 the output of P?S/8 PAL as compared to the output of \f1\tab\f0 PAL8.\f1   [Update: The output of 1988 P?S/8 PAL is now supported to \tab the same extent; the opening content was changed and unified.  \tab What is removed makes sense when dealing with real-world \tab printers; however must be removed for host printing purposes.]\f0\par
\par
5)\f1\tab\f0 The final \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0\f0  and related artifacts are also removed from all\par
 \f1\tab\f0 P?S/8 PAL and PAL8\f1  \f0 output.  This is a bit more complicated than \f1\tab\f0 expected as both assemblers have\f1  \f0 quirks in the output as a \f1\tab\f0 function of specific options selected.  Pragmatic code\f1  \f0 in the \f1\tab\f0 filtering utility handles all known cases.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Note: In the unlikely event a file contains more than a single \f1\tab\f0 trailing \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0  \f0 character, only the last one is removed.  Repeating \f1\tab\f0 the filtering operation on\f1  \f0 the file [as many times as required] \f1\tab\f0 will remove additional trailing \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0  \f0 characters.\f1   PAL8 is \tab especially prone to this when the output is sent to the high-\tab speed punch handler specifically; no case of more than a single \tab extra trailing \highlight2 <FF>\highlight0  character has been observed, but the \tab filtering program is prepared to handle as many as necessary by \tab repeating the filtering process.\f0\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Note: It is theorized the presence of all of these problems is \f1\tab\f0 associated with\f1  \f0 bad documentation and limited contact with line\f1  \tab\f0 printers in general by the PDP-8\f1  \f0 programmers involved with both \f1\tab operating \f0 systems.  Some output techniques used \f1 were merely \tab\f0 mimicking poor output routines written by early hardware\f1\tab\f0 engineers.\par
\f1\par
\tab\f0 The SIMH PDP-8\f1  \f0 simulator environment \f1 tends to \f0 shed light on \f1\tab\f0 all programs using bad techniques.  However,\f1  \f0 some additional \f1\tab\f0 problematic "features" were added to the OS/8 PTP: handler for\par
\f1\tab\f0 dubious purposes; this will be documented in an external "white \f1\tab\f0 paper" document as\f1  \f0 mentioned above.  Regardless of reason, the \f1\tab\f0 filtering utilities remove all known\f1  \f0 problematic cases.\page Additional issues.\par
\par
OS/8 is not capable of producing a direct output of the BITMAP program as part of\f1  \f0 an integrated assembly/map operation.  However, the\f1  \f0 separate output of the BITMAP\f1  \f0 program can be concatenated with the main PAL8 listing file for final printing or\f1  \f0 post-processing [such as creating a composite .PDF file].\par
\par
P?S/8 supports the MAP program [a superset of the \f1 OS/8\f0  BITMAP program] which can be\f1  \f0 chained to by P?S/8 PAL using command-line options.  [In one operation, it is\f1  \f0 possible to assemble, map, load and go as long as there are no assembly errors.]\f1   \f0 As such, the LPT: output from both programs is produced in the same session and\f1  \f0 will be filtered along with the P?S/8 PAL output.\par
\par
Various additional output quirks appear in the MAP output only; as of this writing,\f1  \f0 the filtering utility handles all known problems.\f1   \f0 [Some of the problems\f1  were \f0 remedied in newer releases of the MAP utility.]\par
\par
All trailing \highlight2\f1 <FF>\highlight0\f0  characters are removed from all output; this is consistent with\f1  \f0 host management and post-processing techniques that apply.\par
\par
For those curious to see these problems directly, the \f1 P?S \f0 Advanced SIMH PDP-8 simulator\f1  \f0 package allows viewing the line printer and paper-tape output files; viewing a file\f1  \f0 before applying the filtering utilities will illustrate what problematic quirks\f1  \f0 exist within the file [and then viewing after application of the filtering utilities\par
will show how the output was corrected].\par
\par
P?S/8 Date issues.\par
\par
The basic P?S/8 file system [known as the TFS or Tiny File System directory] does\f1  \f0 not support individual file dates.  However, the operating system supports an\f1  \f0 overall date that ranges from 1960 to \f1 2087\f0  in the original implementatio\f1 n.\par
\par
Note: The date algorithm and base year are being modified for the upcoming release of P?S/8 Version 9A.  The date support is being expanded to handle all dates from 01-Jan-1900 through 31-Dec-2411.  The former modestly tedious maintenance requirement will be completely removed; the entire date will be available to any conforming program directly within the system kernel.\par
\par
In the 1976 implementation of P?S/8 system date support, the low-order portion of the date is implementented as a 12-bit word that can be set by the system DATE utility.  Programs such as P?S/8 PAL and P?S/8 MAP access the current value to provide date support in listing output.\par
\par
However, the high-order portion of the date is implemented by pragmatisms within each program to be coordinated as part of a system maintenance to be performed manually.  Thus, for each program that supports the current date, it will be necessary to perform a minor patch to specific locations within the core-image of each program.\page Unfortunately, the 1976 release was the first implementation of the date support; it is not known whether the date can be updated past 1976 through 1987.  There is no surviving documentation on specifics of this release.\line\line The 1988 release of P?S/8 [Version 8Z] uses the same method of date support and is fully documented.  The release associated with this document has been updated as described below:\par
\par
Each program includes an internal storage location for the date year base.  There is no particular pattern as to where the internal location is located since the matter is generally handled by program initialization code.\par
\par
Note: An associated change log document is part of this package.  All binary patches are logged detailing the exact locations modified; all date-related patches can be found there for future use.  [The next update can be performed 01-Jan-2016; however, it is hoped version 9A of P?S/8 will be available by then.]\par
\par
The value stored in the date year base word is multiplied by eight.  This becomes the high-order portion of a double-precision calculation of any day in the entire date range relative to 01-Jan-1960.\par
\par
For example, in 1960 the value would be 0, in 1968 it would be 1, etc.  For the current base year of 2008, the value should be set to 6.\par
\par
The date can be set to any day in the range of the base date through the end of the eleventh year past; thus, the current release of P?S/8 Version 8Z will allow valid dates from 01-Jan-2008 through 31-Dec-2019.\par
\par
Once the P?S/8 system kernel is updated to support the entire date range in memory, all date-related source code will be modified to conform to the new values; this entire issue will then be obsolete for users of P?S/8 Version 9A.\par
\par
\f0 OS/8 Date issues.\par
\par
OS/8 has a series of \f1 unfixable \f0 date problems:\par
\par
1)\f1\tab\f0 The internal date within OS/8 is limited to 1970-1977.  However, \f1\tab\f0 a kludge exists\f1  \f0 to allow the date to be \b perceived\b0  to be as late \f1\tab\f0 as 2001 [assuming that certain\f1  \f0 program bugs are fixed; as such, \f1\tab\f0 certain outputs will become corrupted if the\f1  \f0 year is set past \f1\tab\f0 1999].\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 The underlying problem is \f1 that \f0 OS/8 stores a 12-bit date word \f1\tab\f0 found in memory\f1  \f0 from the original pre-PS/8 design that was never \f1\tab\f0 updated.\page\f1\tab\f0 Note: Some users\f1  \f0 disable the file date entirely because the\f1\tab\f0 directory structure is so compromised\f1  \f0 that the underlying storage \f1\tab\f0 may become inaccessible as a function of the total\f1  \f0 number of \f1\tab\f0 files and empty areas on the device\f1 ; adding an additional word \tab per file entry reduces the overall number of file entries allowed \tab in the directory.  T\f0 his is a device-wide setting;\f1  \f0 yo\f1 u\f0  \f1 must give \tab up\f0  all \f1 file \f0 dates to regain the space in the directory for more \f1\tab\f0 files\f1  \f0 [or empt\f1 y entries\f0 ].\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 In a\f1  hasty\f0  desperation measure implemented in 1977\f1  after several \tab years of procrastination\f0 , two additional bits were made\f1\tab\f0 available for the overall date; however the additional bits are \f1\tab\f0 implemented in\f1  \f0 memory only.  As such, the date can be set to any \f1\tab\f0 year in the range of\f1  \f0 1970-2001.  However, assuming there is a \f1\tab\f0 date word function in the directory,\f1  \f0 the best that can be \f1\tab\f0 displayed is the \b presumption\b0  that all files are created in\f1  \f0 the \f1\tab\f0 year range of the current year and the previous seven years \f1\tab\f0 regardless of any\f1  \f0 differing reality.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Worse still, this means \f1\tab\f0 that files for the present year will\f1  \tab\f0 appear to be in the future if they are for an apparent date later \f1\tab\f0 in the present\f1  \f0 year than today.  In reality, the file was created \f1\tab\f0 either 8, 16 or 24 years in\f1  \f0 the past, with no way to determine \f1\tab\f0 which specifically applies; the fantasy\f1  \f0 presentation of files \f1\tab\f0 created in the future \f1 would have to\f0  be ignored.\par
\par
2)\f1\tab\f0 The entire kludge breaks down in 2001, the last year the kludge \f1\tab\f0 can be operated.\f1   \f0 Due to program bugs, certain programs will \f1\tab\f0 mishandle dates past 1999.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Note:\f1  \f0 P?S/8 has been tested well past 2000 for format errors \f1\tab\f0 associated with the end of\f1  \f0 a century; OS/8 suffers from the \f1\tab\f0 infamous "Y2K" problem.\par
\par
Date Issue Recourse.\par
\par
It is recommended that files in both\f1  the 1976 release of P?S/8 [Version 8P] and OS/8\f0  be set to 1986.\f1   \f0 Files that are created\par
inside of the SIMH PDP-8 simulator environment can be transmitted to the host\f1  \f0 environment with these dates.  The values can be edited to current years for\f1  \f0 cosmetic consistency.\par
\par
\f1 Other than proper date patch maintenance, there are no such problems in P?S/8 Version 8Z.  Future releases of P?S/8 will require nothing but manually updating of the current date as necessary.\par
\par
Note: Unlike OS/8, P?S/8 retains the current date until changed.\par
\par
Other than changing the date for cosmetic purposes, it is expected the filtering utility will correct all output file quirks created by any of the three supported systems.  The file dates can be updated with trivial TECO macros to find and replace all date strings with more appropriate values as needed.\page Example:\par
\par
