Appendix A.\par
\par
A few comparisons of OS/8 and P?S/8.\par
\par
Most of the information in this section is based on the vast experience of the author of this document, Charles Lasner who is the principal developer of P?S/8.  He has vast experience with OS/8 and has written utilities for OS/8 most notably the Kermit-12 package for the OS/8 family for all PDP-8 models, the PDP-12 and all DECmates.  One of his specialties is writing device handlers for both P?S/8 and OS/8 for various compatible devices sold by third-party companies for various 12-bit computers such as the MDC8 from Computer Extension Systems, Inc [CESI] [including all MDC8 system diagnostics].  Few individuals are in his unique position to provide an informed critique of both operating systems including both shortcomings and strong points where applicable.\par
\par
As of this writing, OS/8 is a totally dead system; no one is developng any system-wide improvements; few, if any, are even possible due to original poor design and management interference.  At best, we can use it in many ways as an example of what \i not\i0  to do.\par
\par
P?S/8 is currently under active development after a period of several decades of stagnation due to personal issues of the author, sufficiently overcome at this stage to allow active pursuit of advancing this system that actually predates OS/8 by several years.\par
\par
Early history of the R-L Monitor System, OS/8 and P?S/8.\par
\par
\f0 P?S/8 is based\f1  in part\f0  on the R-L Monitor System which predates all\f1  single-user PDP-8\f0  DEC operating systems of\f1  any \f0 import\f1 ance\f0 .  It was not well received by DEC management, but was universally loved by\f1  \f0 most of the \f1 DEC \f0 PDP-8 programming group, many of whom met at Brooklyn Polytech in the\f1  \f0 1960's and 1970's.\par
\par
\f1 The R-L Monitor system was written by some of the best PDP-8 programmers ever, most notably Richard Lary and Lenny Elekman.  Their numerous accomplishments include POLY BASIC, later sold by DEC as EDU-System 30 and EDU-System 15/30 [for the TD8E only] and various other projects.  Individually, Lenny Elekman is the author of 4K BASIC, which is the basis for 8K BASIC, various components of OS/8 BASIC in conjunction with others, and FOCAL-12 for DIAL-MS.  DIAL-MS was written by one of their colleagues Jack Burness, who in developed 8K BASIC from 4K BASIC.  These extraordinary programmers also worked with their friend and colleague Hank Maurer, the original developer of DIBOL-8, the precursor of COS-300/310.\par
\par
Richard Lary is also the original producer of the demo version of what later became PS/8 [later renamed OS/8 because marketing complained about having to sell a "programming system" instead of an "operating system" thus more appropriate system name, at least according to the salesmen of the time.]\f3\page\cf3\f1 Appendix A [continued].\par
\par
\cf0 Although never officially acknowledged, it is true that the R-L Monitor System with hacked-up command names was the actual release of the original DIBOL-8; the only new contribution was the DIBOL compiler and run-time system added by Hank Maurer.  The command changes were made to be more compatible with the paper specification of COS-300 which was not quite yet under development.\par
\par
Clearly, the R-L Monitor System plays an important role in the history of DEC operating systems, even if not documented outside of the small circle of PDP-8 programmers who were personal friends of each other.\par
\par
\f0 OS/8 is a mediocre system that was demonstrated to DEC management\f1  by Richard Lary\f0  as a weekend hack,\f1  \f0 but was a prematurely fixated design with many\f1  \f0 shortcomings; as such, it is mediocre\f1  \f0 at best and eventually much of the design limitations caught up with it.  There are\f1  \f0 problems such as poor directory design and an unworkable date methodology that ran\f1  \f0 out in 1977\f1 ,\f0  but was kludged to appear to work to the uninformed until perhaps as\f1  \f0 late as 1999 or in some cases 2001 depending on how many fixable bugs were still\f1  \f0 present.\par
\par
\f1 It is most important to know that OS/8 as written was intended only as a demo for another system to be written from scratch, including a lot of input from the prevailing PDP-8 expert community at the time, including Charles Lasner, who was asked to work on the project and refused; instead he worked with other well-known PDP-8 expert programmers on making improvements to the R-L Monitor System.\par
\par
Note: The week-end hack that eventually became OS/8 was known to PDP-8 programmers as the First Upward-Compatible Keyboard Editor and Monitor [with the obvious somewhat offensive acronym].  It was to be a demo for a true replacement system based on PDP-8 community input to be known as the Second Upward-Compatible Keyboard Editor and Monitor [with the similar acronym].  Had the second system been designed and implemented, it is entirely possible that Charles Lasner would have been one of its architects.  [In certain ways, the emerging P?S/8 SHELL project is the fulfillment of this never-developed product.]\par
\par
Had Richard Lary been treated at the time as the expert he is, OS/8 as we know it would have been totally scrapped.  However, DEC management has always been the weak spot [and ultimate downfall] of the company.\par
\par
Most notably, of the five original PDP-8 pioneering experts [Richard Lary, Lenny Elekman, Jack Burness, Hank Maurer and Joe Fischetti], all of the people on the list other than Richard Lary refused to work on the project Richard Lary was virtually forced to continue despite it not really being his intention either.  [Charles Lasner was asked after the others refused to deal with it; he sided with the majority.]\page\cf3 Appendix A [continued].\par
\cf0\par
Despite widespread conventional wisdom to the contrary, Richard Lary's participation in OS/8 virtually ended early on, other than to periodically participate with others on certain projects such as TECO for OS/8 and Fortran IV's runtime system to allow simulation of the FPP hardware when necessary.  Unfortunately, most of the work was done by mediocre people and thus OS/8 is largely a mediocre system based on design elements never really thought through.\cf3\par
\par
\cf0 In many ways, \f0 OS/8 was figuratively painted into a corner due to the poor design of both the\f1  \f0 directory and the basics of the system resident kernel that are unfixable without\f1  \f0 essentially starting all over; this has been deemed not worth bothering with by\f1  \f0 prevailing PDP-8 developers\f1  such as Charles Lasner.\par
\par
\f0 As the list of PDP-8 developers dwindles to a small few, it is clear that the future\f1  \f0 rests with developing the descendent of the R-L\f1  \f0 Monitor System.  This system has\f1  \f0 always been known as P?S/8, a pun on the duality of a) The members of the PDP-8\f1  \f0 programming world at large that met at Brooklyn Poly call\f1 ing\f0  themselves the Poly\f1  \f0 Question Society [always abbreviated P?S if possible, or else PQS if in a limited\f1  \f0 character set situation], b) DEC\f1 's\f0  marketing penchant for forming\f1  \f0 product names\f1  \f0 suggestive of some ethereal system coupled with a suffix for the machine number.\par
\par
\f1 Note: Certain members of the P?S submitted the R-L Monitor System to DECUS using the name MS/8 to further mock DEC marketing tactics.  The R-L Monitor System can only run on a single TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape drive unit [0].  If an image of this system is made available as a .tu56 SIMH standard image, it should run under the SIMH PDP-8 simulator.\par
\par
\f3 Development of P?S/8 and some OS/8 issues.\par
\par
\f0 P?S/8 is all new work based on the concepts started in the excellent base system\f1  \f0 that is the R-L Monitor System.  The R-L Monitor System was created under incredible\f1  \f0 conditions, literally being hand\f1 -\f0 assembled code that was manually toggled into the\f1  \f0 PDP-8 using nothing other than the PDP-8 front panel and various sets of human\f1  \f0 fingers over a course of months.\par
\par
Note: Charles Lasner, the principle author of\f1  \f0 P?S/8, this package and this document, personally repaired the computer subjected to\f1  \f0 this abuse.  Literally, every front-panel slide switch was carefully replaced as\f1  \f0 they all had become quite unreliable; these switches are simply not designed for\f1  \f0 this class of rough service.\par
\cf3\f1\par
\cf0\f0 P?S/8 is still under development as of the writing of this document.  As with the\f1  \f0 R-L Monitor before it, DEC managers were given two opportunities to review P?S/8\f1  \f0 for the purpose of DEC taking it over for the "price" of hardware donation to\f1  \f0 Brooklyn Polytech for the benefit of students.\page\cf3\f1 Appendix A [continued].\par
\par
\cf0\f0 Note: Many large corporations\f1  \f0 routinely do this to lower their taxes, thus the actual cost to DEC conceivably\f1  \f0 could have been negligible or actually profitable.  At the time of these\f1  \f0 "negotiations", members of the P?S still visited the Brooklyn Polytech\f1  facility\f0  unofficially,\f1  \f0 giving advice and guidance to yet another generation of programmers.  Charles Lasner\f1  \f0 and others are considered the "second generation" of PDP-8 programmers who learned\f1  \f0 how to program the PDP-8 using the R-L monitor system instead of having to abuse the\f1  \f0 front panel.  As such, they had an opportunity to critique the R-L Monitor System\f1  \f0 and then create improvements and new features over the course of many years of\f1  \f0 intermittent development.\par
\par
P?S/8 was never written as a pa\f1 id\f0  project;\f1  \f0 programmers have to seek employment by writing applications, not operating systems;\f1  \f0 by and large, clients were unaware of exactly how these projects got\f1  \f0 developed;\f1  \f0 impetus for some of the P?S/8 development stemmed from the need to accomplish work\f1  \f0 commitments to clients because there was no other way to accomplish \f1 what was required\f0 .  For\f1  \f0 certain DEC employees, writing an operating system component or two was the entirety\f1  \f0 of their work.\f1   [At times, there are advantages to working in "the real world".]\par
\par
Earlier releases of this package contained only P?S/8 Version 8P released circa 1976.  \f0 More development work was performed on P?S/8 for as much as a decade past the\f1  \f0 release of\f1  this earlier work.\par
\par
New to this package is the release of P?S/8 Version 8Z for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56.  This is essentially the system released first on 23-Feb-1988 with some binary patches applied to fix several minor known bugs.  A change log file will be kept on P?S/8 development starting in 2015; it is anticipated a newer release [Version 9A] will be available after some work on the source files has been integrated into the general release.\par
\par
\f0 At some point, work on the basic P?S/8 system will \f1 virtually\f0  cease, having taken\f1  \f0 it as far as it can go short of adding additional system programs [which will\f1  \f0 continue to be added].\par
\par
The future of P?S/8 is\f1  the inclusion of a\f0 n optional \f1 S\f0 HELL overlay which will be geared\f1  \f0 towards larger memory computers than the basic 4K required by the R-L Monitor System\f1  \f0 and P?S/8 as in the current implementation.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 requires only 4K; however,\f1  \f0 certain system programs require more memory to either improve performance, add\f1  \f0 additional capabilities or both, most notably P?S/8 PAL which can utilize as much\f1  \f0 as 20K of memory to support humongous assemblies with huge symbol tables.  Few and\f1  \f0 far between one encounters PDP-8 projects that need that much memory to assemble the\f1  \f0 programs; the actual working binary programs could require  up to 32K regardless of\f1  \f0 how many user symbols are in the source code.\page\cf3\f1 Appendix A [continued].\par
\par
\cf0\f0 Certain P?S/8 configurations require extended memory to operate due to the\f1  \f0 limitations of certain devices used to create the system resident kernel.  Within\f1  \f0 the first 4K of memory, the requirements are met in all systems.  However, certain\f1  system \f0 device handlers require an additional section of code resident in the highest\f1  \f0 available memory field [which must be in the range of field 1 through\f1  field\f0  7]\f1 .\f0  However,\f1  \f0 assuming more than 8K is available, \f1 any \f0 user \f1 or system] \f0 program\f1  \f0 is allowed\f1  \f0 to allocate 100% of the extended memory\f1  below the field of the handler extension\f0  without restrictions [unlike OS/8 which\f1  \f0 always reserves the last page of every field due to poor design].\par
\par
The SHELL will be familiar to any seasoned DEC user; the user interface goals are\f1  \f0 very much like DEC systems created after OS/8 where the notion of CCL is not\f1  \f0 considered an option [that might have to be disabled].\par
\par
Note: OS/8 is slavishly\f1  \f0 designed to match the capabilities of TOPS-10 from the days before CCL was created.\f1   \f0 CCL was added to TOPS-10 and then was sloppily added to OS/8 in a way that\f1  \f0 theoretically seems appropriate.  \f1 P\f0 roblem\f1 s stem from the\f0  high-overhead process\f1  \f0 of software faking out other software which is profoundly inefficient and\f1  \f0 unnecessary in an alternate [better thought out] design.  It is rather curious that\f1  \f0 the use of CCL [not necessarily named CCL, sometimes known as DCL] was universally\f1  \f0 encouraged within DEC with the lone exception of OS/8: Unless the system device was\f1  \f0 powerful enough, it was recommended CCL be disabled due to \f1 the serious\f0  overhead when used\par
on common slower devices such as DECtape, a configuration that P?S/8 thrives on due\f1  \f0 to superior design.\par
\par
\f1 T\f0 he \f1 P?S/8 \f0 SHELL will allow CCL-like commands to be entered directly without the need\f1  \f0 for a clumsy intermediate command-decode mode.\f1   The goal will be performance first and slavish compatibility second, although the command structure will be quite familiar to anyone with a background in any DEC system based on either CCL or DCL.\par
\par
\f0 Note: For backward-compatibility,\f1  \f0 the decode function will be used in the very few programs [such as PIP] where it\f1  \f0 makes sense to do so.  However, the function may be so specifically designed, each\f1  \f0 program requiring a command mode may implement its own variant; it is likely few\f1  \f0 programs will completely agree upon specific requirements.\f1  \f0 However, the SHELL\f1  \f0 implementation will include support for various forms of commands not necessarily\f1  \f0 designed to be compatible with the SHELL file\f1  \f0 system conventions.  This will allow\f1  \f0 "foreign" file\f1  \f0 systems to be supported, along with other variations known to be\f1  \f0 required by certain programs such as passing option strings not syntactically\par
processed by the command decoder.\page\cf3\f1 Appendix A [continued].\par
\cf0\f0\par
In some cases, OS/8 has some awkward usage of the command-decode function which was\f1  \f0 not designed to provide what is required for certain specific programs; bad kludges\f1  \f0 only allow barely adequate functionality because the command-decode function has\f1  \f0 arbitrary rules regarding command formation contrary to the needs of certain\f1  \f0 programs calling it.  The alternative would be to "reinvent the wheel" which would\f1  \f0 point out the futility of the design.  A heavy-handed approach was used to achieve a\f1  \f0 program that barely worked with unnecessary restrictions and high overhead.\par
\par
Note:\f1  \f0 In the P?S/8 SHELL environment, this sort of inappropriate mechanism will be\f1  \f0 unnecessary as commands can be processed in various ways to suit the needs of a\f1  \f0 variety of utilities; much of the basic\par
P?S/8 command processor already functions in\f1  \f0 this manner; the SHELL will implement a superset of what the basic system\f1  \f0 already\f1  \f0 supports; for commands where OS/8 struggles, P?S/8 already has several analogous\par
commands implemented with \f1 little to \f0 no overhead.\par
\par
\f1 The following is an example of awkward OS/8 command implementation:\f0\par
\par
The RX02 double-density diskette controller requires a rewrite of the data sectors\f1  \f0 to change the diskette functionality from single to double density or vice versa.\f1   \f0 This is sometimes erroneously referred to as a \i format\i0  operation.  In actuality\f1 , \f0 true formatting is impossible on all DEC RX01/02/03 family drives\f1  due to hardware limitations\f0 .\par
\par
